Read docs/fleet-architecture.md §0 (the north star: human interventions per agent-hour) and §5 (Home's health strip); docs/design.md §15 (the scorecard: autonomy's interventions (a) to (d)); crates/spawn-postgres/sql/schema.sql (`spawn_commands`: principal, session, action, state, succeeded, created and finished times); crates/spawn-postgres/src/fleet.rs `FLEET_HEALTH` (`ladder_24h.human`, `agent_hours_24h`, `human_per_agent_hour`); crates/spawn-postgres/src/scorecard.rs and crates/spawn-cli/src/scorecard/history.rs (how autonomy counts a human command, at its creation); objective 043's views (`spawn_decisions`, `spawn_interventions`) and `/api/scorecard/fleet`, which come before this one; crates/spawn-cli/src/serve.rs (the read-only routes, BAD_REQUEST for a 400); web/src/fleet.ts, web/src/components/fleet/HealthStrip.tsx and FleetView.tsx (Home).

The problem. The north star counts commands, not what a person meant. A refused command and its retries each count as an intervention. In the week to 2026-11-11 the live fleet recorded 228 human commands. 100 of them were one owner note: it was refused 99 times while the pane was "Busy", then typed once (2026-11-06 05:14-05:43). Two more notes were sent 36 and 20 times the same way. So interventions per agent-hour read about 1.6 where about 1 was meant. No page shows which interventions a figure is made of, so nobody can see that.

Required behaviour. (a) One intent, from the records alone (never from the text's meaning). A person's command (principal `human` or `spawn`, the board's and the CLI's) continues the intent of the latest earlier command with the same principal, the same `session_id` and an equal `action` (the whole JSON, compared as jsonb) when two things hold: that earlier command finished failed (`succeeded` false) at or before this one was created, and this one was created at most 60 minutes after that failure. Otherwise it starts a new intent. An intent's time is its first command's creation, its commands are the chain in order, and its outcome is its last command's: `taken` (succeeded), `failed`, or `pending` (not finished). The same text sent again after it was taken is a new intent, and so is a send to another session, by another principal, or with another action. A view `spawn_human_intents` (readable by the reader and the supervisor) has one row per intent: `at_ms`, `intent` (its first command's id), `principal`, `agent_id`, `session_id`, `commands` (bigint[], in order), `attempts`, `outcome`.

(b) Everywhere Spawn counts a person's commands as interventions or human decisions, it counts intents:
- the scorecard's autonomy kind `command` (`spawn scorecard --json` and `/api/scorecard`; design §15 (a) is updated to say so), which gains the part `retries` (the windowed intents' commands beyond each intent's first);
- `/api/fleet`'s `ladder_24h.human` and `human_per_agent_hour`, both of principal `human` or `spawn`;
- 043's `spawn_interventions` (kind `command`: one row per intent, at its time) and `spawn_decisions` (tier `human`: one row per intent), and through them `/api/scorecard/fleet`.
Interventions of the other kinds (resume, owner task, cancel-and-reassign) are counted as before.

(c) `GET /api/interventions?window=24h|7d[&until=<RFC 3339>]` answers 400 for any other window or a bad `until`. It covers the window [until − window, until), `until` defaulting to the database's now, with each command's outcome as it stood before `until`, and returns JSON:
- `window`, `since_ms`, `until_ms`;
- `agent_hours`: the executions' running time clipped to the window, one still running counted to `until`, to 3 decimals;
- `total`: the interventions, as the scorecard's autonomy counts them for the same window;
- `by_kind` {`command`, `resume`, `owner_task`, `cancel_reassign`} and `retries`;
- `per_agent_hour`: `total` over `agent_hours` to 3 decimals, or null with no agent hours;
- `items`: newest first, one per intervention. Each item has `kind`, `at_ms`, `principal` (for a command), `agent` and `session` (for a command), `line` (a command's text: the first line of an input, at most 120 characters, else the action's `type`; for a resume "resumed mission #<id>"; for an owner task "assigned task #<id>"; for a cancel-and-reassign "cancelled task #<id>, assigned task #<id>"), `commands` (an intent's command ids in order; [] for the other kinds), `attempts` and `outcome` (a command's; null for the other kinds).

(d) Home shows the north star for 24 h and for 7 d: per agent-hour, the count and the retries it left out, and a list of the interventions behind each figure. web/src/interventions.ts exports `interventionsView(answer)` →
- `{figure, count, retries, rows}`:
  - `figure`: `per_agent_hour` to 3 decimals ("0.400"), "—" when null;
  - `count`: "1 intervention" or "<n> interventions";
  - `retries`: null for none, "1 retry not counted" or "<n> retries not counted";
  - `rows`: one per item, in order.
- Each row is `{kind, who, line, commands, outcome}`:
  - `who`: a command's agent, else "";
  - `commands`: the ids as "#<id>" joined by ", ", then for an intent of more than one attempt " (<n> tries)"; "" for the other kinds;
  - `outcome`: the item's outcome or "".
interventions.ts and everything it imports must load with node alone: type stripping, no bundler, no packages, relative imports that name their `.ts` file, `import type` for anything used only as a type, and only erasable TypeScript (no enum, namespace or parameter property).

The gate judges this with rsi/scenarios/049.sh (its header holds every number, worked by hand). It seeds a fresh database with no loop, as 043 does: commands, executions and one mission's resume, at hours before a fixed U.
- (1) `/api/interventions` for 24 h and 7 d gives the hand-worked figures and items, each intent's command ids exact, and 400 for an unknown window.
- (2) Every figure equals the scenario's own SQL recount of rule (a) and of the agent hours.
- (3) The scorecard's autonomy, given the same windows, counts the same interventions and retries.
- (4) `/api/fleet`'s `ladder_24h.human` is the 24 h intents, and `human_per_agent_hour` equals them over its own `agent_hours_24h`.
- (5) 043's views, which exist once 043 has landed (this objective comes after it), count intents.
- (6) `interventionsView` under node gives the page's words.
It fails at (1) on ea3a2e9f: there is no `/api/interventions`.

What it saves. The owner reads one number that means what it says: how often a person had to step in, per hour of agent work, over a day and a week. A click on it shows exactly which interventions it is made of, so a spike reads as "one note retried 100 times" or "ten real decisions" without a query.

Rules: never edit the protected paths, which the gate rejects: rsi/, scripts/ (scripts/rsi/, scripts/blackbox/, scripts/e2e.sh among them), deploy/, all of web/ except web/src/, web/test/ and web/index.html, rust-toolchain files and .cargo/. The gate also rejects fewer runnable tests than the base, any lint relaxation and any build-time code. The gate builds offline from the cargo caches already on the host. Keep every existing check green and add tests of your own for the new behaviour.
